# Coding Standards for Rust

## Key Principles

- adhere to [Rust's naming conventions](https://rust-lang.github.io/api-guidelines/naming.html)
- embrace Rust's ownership and type system
- the standard library provides a great reference for best-practices

## Tooling

- use or create `rust-toolchain.toml` files
- before treating Rust changes as done, check the general linting, formatting and documentation

### General Linting

- use `clippy` for general linting tasks
- check against linting issues with `cargo clippy --quiet --bins --tests --benches --no-deps`
- respect all lints defined in `Cargo.toml`
- use `#[expect(...)]` instead of `#[allow(...)]`

### Formatting

- use `rustfmt` for formatting-related tasks
- format the code with `cargo fmt --all`
- check the formatting with `cargo fmt --all --quiet --message-format=human --check`

### Documentation

- use `cargo doc` for documentation-related tasks
- check the documentation with `cargo doc --quiet --all-features --no-deps --document-private-items`
- use intra-doc links

## Paradigms

### Error Handling

- embrace `Result` and `Option` for error handling
- use `?` operator to propagate errors, especially in async functions
- consider implementing custom error types using `::thiserror` for more descriptive errors
- consider using `anyhow` for non-performance critical error handling

### Asynchronous Programming

- prefer `async fn` over `fn -> impl Future`
- use `.await` responsibly, ensuring safe points for context switching
- prefer bounded channels for backpressure; handle capacity limits gracefully
- leverage [`tokio`] (an async runtime) for concurrency
  - use `::tokio::sync::Mutex` and `::tokio::sync::RwLock` for shared state across tasks instead of their standard-library counterparts
  - use `::tokio::task::yield_now` to yield control in cooperative multitasking scenarios
  - write unit tests with `tokio::test` for async tests

